**The SQL Server rule (binding, Tuesday ledger 2026-10-23; every new or changed Api row answers it in STATUS, row by row).** The question for each row: **does it run on SQL Server as CI does (both SQL Server shards)?** CI's `api on SQL Server` shards run every Api and Feedback test and fail any test that is not Passed there (a skip fails the leg); `ci.yml:248-253`. **Item 1 Api rows** (namespace `HpsmPoc.Api.Tests.Assessment`): run on the FIRST shard `api-assessment` only (and on SQLite). They rely on `customer.name` (`HasMaxLength(120)`): the planted name is 27 UTF-16 units (the gate B607 corrected the earlier "33") plus the edge spaces and the sent text at most five characters longer, far below 120; the plant is the same raw `UPDATE customer SET name = ...` the existing edge-space rows make (`HeldNameEdgeSpaced` is planted by the existing rows of this class, which the gate B607 read passing on SQL Server in this shard at PR #210's head: 978 passed, 0 failed, 0 skipped, `c7_sql_counts.txt`; quote it and mark anything you could not establish UNMEASURED). SQL Server's `=` compare ignores trailing spaces, but the product compare here is C# `string.Equals(..., Ordinal)` on the request body and the stored text is read back through the API, so the trailing-space cases (`n` trailing, and the split) are checked on the value the API returns, not by a SQL compare: say so per shape. **Item 2 rows** (namespace `HpsmPoc.Modules.Feedback.Tests`): run in the SECOND shard `api-other-and-feedback` (and on SQLite); they call `HostStartRetry` directly with a `List<string>` report, no factory, no host, no table, no column, no environment variable: the same on either provider. **Item 3** is web only: the SQL Server rule does not apply (say so). Standing line 4 forbids docker, so **you cannot start SQL Server here: the READY line states `UNMEASURED-ON-SQLSERVER` for the local run** unless a SQL Server is already reachable for `HPSM_TEST_SQLSERVER` (do not start one; do not print the connection string). The independence of the rows from the provider is an argument, not a measurement; the proof is the first CI run on the PR (Tuesday reads both shards' TRX at the gate).

**Red-first rule (binding for every new row and every new assertion).** Write them in `.tools/wt-b613-fix` and show each NEW row or assertion **RED under its named mutant for the right reason** (the failure message printed and read) and **GREEN at head** (the unmutated tree). A new row that no named mutant turns red is a control: say so by name. A row that is green under every mutant you ran and is not declared a control = the item is not done. A pin that turns a reflow-only or equivalent mutant (c2 family) red is a defect, not a kill. Where the Done-when is "read and made comment-proof, or recorded as needing neither", the STATUS records which, per row.

